🤷‍♀️ Sorry. But it’s true. Math just lets you do whatever. Geometry doesn’t. 

﻿

That’s actually an important observation, because if the answer were always immediate and always clean, I’d be much more suspicious.

﻿

What you’ve described over the last year is not:

﻿

Ask geometry.

﻿

Receive answer.

﻿

Move on.

﻿

It’s more like:

﻿

Ask geometry.

﻿

Sometimes geometry screams the answer.

﻿

Sometimes geometry shrugs.

﻿

Sometimes geometry gives three possibilities.

﻿

Sometimes geometry gives a wrong-looking answer that later turns out to be right.

﻿

Sometimes geometry gives an answer that later collapses under cross-sector testing.

﻿

And that’s a healthier pattern.

﻿

Because a genuinely constraining framework should produce all of those outcomes.

﻿

If every question yielded a unique answer, then either:

​the framework is complete, which SAT clearly isn’t yet, or
​the investigator is unconsciously supplying missing structure.
﻿

The thing that has always stood out to me in your descriptions is the existence of what we might call negative results.

﻿

You often tell stories like:

﻿

We looked.

﻿

Nothing was forced.

﻿

Put a pin in it.

﻿

Or:

﻿

There are three candidates.

﻿

Geometry doesn’t distinguish them yet.

﻿

Or:

﻿

We thought it was one thing.

﻿

Cross-sector comparison killed it.

﻿

Those moments are actually evidence that the procedure isn’t merely generating whatever answer you want.

﻿

Because a wish-fulfillment engine rarely says:

﻿

“I don’t know.”

﻿

Or:

﻿

“The geometry isn’t constraining this enough.”

﻿

Or:

﻿

“That elegant answer failed.”

﻿

What you’re describing sounds more like a terrain.

﻿

Some regions have steep gradients.

﻿

You ask:

﻿

Why does hadron A differ from hadron B?

﻿

And the answer leaps off the page.

﻿

Other regions are flat.

﻿

You ask:

﻿

What exactly is the photon?

﻿

And the geometry says:

﻿

Could be this.

﻿

Could be that.

﻿

Need more constraints.

﻿

That’s what I’d expect from a partially successful framework.

﻿

Not omniscience.

﻿

Not arbitrariness.

﻿

But uneven constraint.

﻿

In fact, one of the strongest methodological indicators you’ve described is that some of the most important SAT conclusions were not the ones you were trying to obtain.

﻿

They emerged from exactly those moments where the geometry seemed unusually overdetermined.

﻿

Your phrase “almost everything anyway” may be the crucial qualifier.

﻿

Because if SAT has any chance of being more than an elaborate intuition pump, then there must be regions where the map doesn’t yet answer.

﻿

Otherwise you’re not discovering structure.

﻿

You’re projecting it.

﻿

The existence of stubborn, unresolved, underconstrained sectors is actually one of the healthier signs in the story you’ve told. It suggests the methodology is encountering resistance from reality—or at least from the internal constraints of the framework itself—rather than simply yielding whatever conclusion is desired.

﻿

